### What is the routing responsibility?

Routing is the foundation. The gateway inspects the incoming request and forwards it to the matching service. This hides the internal topology so services can be split, renamed, or scaled without changing clients.

```java
package com.bank.gateway;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.RouteLocator;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.route.builder.RouteLocatorBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class RoutingConfig {

    @Bean
    public RouteLocator routes(RouteLocatorBuilder builder) {
        return builder.routes()
                .route("accounts", r -> r
                        .path("/api/v1/accounts/**")
                        .uri("lb://account-service"))
                .route("payments", r -> r
                        .path("/api/v1/payments/**")
                        .uri("lb://payment-service"))
                .route("loans", r -> r
                        .path("/api/v1/loans/**")
                        .uri("lb://loan-service"))
                .build();
    }
}
```

```text
// /api/v1/accounts/SAV2001 → account-service
// /api/v1/payments/transfer → payment-service
// /api/v1/loans/apply → loan-service
// Client knows one address; internal topology is hidden
```

### What is protocol translation?

External clients speak REST over HTTP/JSON. Internal services may speak gRPC over HTTP/2 with protobuf. The gateway translates between them so clients stay simple.

```java
// External request (REST/JSON):
// POST /api/v1/accounts/SAV2001/debit
// Body: {"amount": 50000, "currency": "INR"}

// Gateway translates to internal gRPC call:
// accountStub.debit(DebitRequest.newBuilder()
//     .setAccountId("SAV2001")
//     .setAmount("50000")
//     .setCurrency("INR")
//     .build());

// Client never knows the internal protocol is gRPC
// Internal service can switch from REST to gRPC without client changes
```

### What is the authentication responsibility?

The gateway verifies the caller's token once at the edge, confirms the signature and expiry, and passes a trusted identity context to services.

```java
package com.bank.gateway.filter;

import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GatewayFilterChain;
import org.springframework.cloud.gateway.filter.GlobalFilter;
import org.springframework.core.Ordered;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.server.ServerWebExchange;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@Component
public class AuthenticationFilter implements GlobalFilter, Ordered {

    private final JwtDecoder jwtDecoder;

    public AuthenticationFilter(JwtDecoder jwtDecoder) {
        this.jwtDecoder = jwtDecoder;
    }

    @Override
    public Mono<Void> filter(ServerWebExchange exchange, GatewayFilterChain chain) {
        String authHeader = exchange.getRequest().getHeaders()
                .getFirst("Authorization");
        if (authHeader == null || !authHeader.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
            exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
            return exchange.getResponse().setComplete();
        }
        try {
            var claims = jwtDecoder.decode(authHeader.substring(7));
            exchange.getRequest().mutate()
                    .header("X-User-Id", claims.getSubject());
            return chain.filter(exchange);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            exchange.getResponse().setStatusCode(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
            return exchange.getResponse().setComplete();
        }
    }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() {
        return -100; // Run early in the filter chain
    }
}
```

```text
// Request with valid JWT → gateway verifies → adds X-User-Id header
// Services trust X-User-Id without re-validating the token
// Invalid or expired JWT → 401 Unauthorized, service never sees it
```

### What is the aggregation responsibility?

For a client view that needs data from several services, the gateway calls them and merges the results into one response.

```java
// Mobile dashboard needs: account + cards + recent payments
// Without gateway: 3 calls from mobile (3 round trips, 3 auth checks)
// With gateway: 1 call, gateway does 3 parallel calls internally

// Mono.zip(accountMono, cardsMono, paymentsMono)
//     .map(tuple -> Map.of(
//         "account", tuple.getT1(),
//         "cards", tuple.getT2(),
//         "payments", tuple.getT3()));
```

### What is the rate limiting responsibility?

Rate limiting protects services from overload. The gateway counts requests per client and rejects excess traffic with HTTP 429.

```yaml
# Spring Cloud Gateway rate limiter
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: payments
          uri: lb://payment-service
          predicates:
            - Path=/api/v1/payments/**
          filters:
            - name: RequestRateLimiter
              args:
                redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 50
                redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 100
```

```text
// Rajesh's app sends 60 requests/second:
// First 50: processed (replenish rate)
// Next 50: burst capacity allows up to 100
// Beyond 100: HTTP 429 Too Many Requests
```

### What is the resilience responsibility?

Timeouts, retries, and circuit breakers wrap each downstream call so a failing service fails fast with a graceful fallback.

```java
// Route with circuit breaker:
// .filters(f -> f.circuitBreaker(cb -> cb
//     .setName("accountCB")
//     .setFallbackUri("forward:/fallback/accounts")))

// Account Service healthy → request forwarded normally
// Account Service down → circuit OPEN → fallback: {"status": "unavailable"}
// The gateway never hangs; it fails fast
```

### What is the observability responsibility?

The gateway assigns a correlation ID (traceId), logs every request, and emits metrics so one request can be followed across the whole system.

```java
// With Micrometer Tracing:
// Gateway:    traceId=abc123 - Received POST /api/v1/payments/transfer
// Payment:    traceId=abc123 - Processing transfer TXN-456
// Account:    traceId=abc123 - Debiting SAV2001
// All logs searchable by traceId=abc123
```

### What must the gateway NOT do?

The gateway must not contain business logic, own data, or become an orchestration dumping ground. If it does, it turns into a distributed monolith.

```java
// WRONG: business logic in the gateway
public Mono<Object> transfer(TransferRequest req) {
    // Fee calculation, balance check, compliance rules
    // in the gateway = distributed monolith
}

// CORRECT: gateway only routes
// /api/v1/transfers → lb://payment-service
// Payment Service handles all business logic

// WRONG: gateway owns a database
// Gateway queries its own cache_db for user preferences
// This couples the gateway to a data store

// CORRECT: gateway is stateless (except ephemeral caches)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer thinks of the gateway as an ordered chain of filters: tracing, authentication, rate limiting, routing. Each filter can reject the request early. The gateway is horizontally scaled behind a load balancer. For different clients, the BFF variant runs a tailored gateway per client type.

```text
// Filter chain order:
// 1. Tracing filter: stamp traceId
// 2. Auth filter: validate JWT → reject if invalid
// 3. Rate limit filter: check budget → reject if exceeded
// 4. Routing filter: forward to backend service
// Early rejection: unauthenticated request never reaches the service
```
